WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:05.100
Speaker 1: OK, let's get into audience questions
for the week, the first question is, David,

00:00:05.100 --> 00:00:11.420
why did so many more minority voters vote
Trump in 2020?

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.900
These are very interesting numbers and we've
talked about a little bit of the why.

00:00:14.900 --> 00:00:22.800
But as a reminder, from 2016 to 2020, Trump
improved six points with black men, three

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:28.720
points with white women, five points with
black women, four points with Hispanic men,

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:34.360
five points with Hispanic women, and then
plus seven with Asians and other minority

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.360
ethnicities.

00:00:35.360 --> 00:00:38.110
So I think that there's a couple of different
things going on.

00:00:38.110 --> 00:00:42.370
Some of it is strictly mathematical and some
of it is more ideological.

00:00:42.370 --> 00:00:45.230
The first let's go to the ideological part
first.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:52.820
I've pointed out that in the twenty twenty
campaign, because of the pandemic, Trump getting

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:58.460
covid the focus being Biden and his socialism
and his dementia and all of these different

00:00:58.460 --> 00:01:05.780
things, by by coincidence, not because Trump
decided I'm going to be less racist.

00:01:05.780 --> 00:01:09.790
This this you know, I'm not going to talk
about Mexican rapists and all this stuff,

00:01:09.790 --> 00:01:16.370
but just because Trump was busy with other
things, he was less overtly antagonistic to

00:01:16.370 --> 00:01:20.860
minorities other than the whole China virus
thing, which resonated with a lot of people,

00:01:20.860 --> 00:01:21.860
actually.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:24.920
So that it's not clear that that really did
him much harm.

00:01:24.920 --> 00:01:31.320
And so by just being distracted with other
things, there was less of the overt xenophobia

00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:33.740
from Trump in twenty twenty than in twenty
sixteen.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:35.010
There wasn't none of it.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:39.830
And and you can still find many examples,
but there was less of it overtly, which probably

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:41.720
had some factor.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:44.810
But I believe that the answer to how did Trump
gain with minority voters?

00:01:44.810 --> 00:01:48.909
I think it's actually more of a mathematical
thing.

00:01:48.909 --> 00:01:55.250
Voter turnout in general was much higher in
twenty twenty about six or seven full points

00:01:55.250 --> 00:01:56.250
higher.

00:01:56.250 --> 00:01:57.920
That's millions of people.

00:01:57.920 --> 00:02:04.799
And it's less in my mind that Trump converted
minority voters that voted for Hillary in

00:02:04.799 --> 00:02:08.009
2016 and they voted for Trump in twenty twenty.

00:02:08.009 --> 00:02:14.430
It's that a ton of every demographic group
didn't vote at all in 2016.

00:02:14.430 --> 00:02:21.630
And from that group, Trump was able to get
people out in some particular districts, areas

00:02:21.630 --> 00:02:22.720
and states.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:27.640
So when I look at the numbers and we have
this idea, there are people who say people

00:02:27.640 --> 00:02:31.780
on the right who say the real racists are
the Democrats.

00:02:31.780 --> 00:02:35.470
And over time, minority voters are going to
realize this.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:38.240
They're going to move to the Republican Party.

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:42.319
And and some of those people are saying these
numbers are evidence of it.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.750
The fact that Trump gained six points with
black men and five points with black women,

00:02:46.750 --> 00:02:47.750
etc.

00:02:47.750 --> 00:02:56.880
The other side of it is that this is a one
time sort of an anomalous or anomalous situation

00:02:56.880 --> 00:03:03.200
and that it's really just because turnout
was particularly high for everybody.

00:03:03.200 --> 00:03:06.910
And Joe Biden's margin of victory is what
we should be paying attention to, which was

00:03:06.910 --> 00:03:08.510
a really big margin of victory.

00:03:08.510 --> 00:03:10.739
I think we just can't resolve it in one election
cycle.

00:03:10.739 --> 00:03:16.900
I think that you have to wait and see what
happens in future election cycles.

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.440
And I don't think it's completely resolved.

00:03:18.440 --> 00:03:25.560
And we're actually going to talk in the next
question about college educated whites leaving

00:03:25.560 --> 00:03:26.560
the Republican Party.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:27.910
Like, are they actually leaving?

00:03:27.910 --> 00:03:28.910
Is it a realignment?

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:29.910
What does it mean?

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:33.520
A lot of these things are more than one election
cycle.

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:39.450
There's one other hypothesis, which is one
that the right likes a lot of kind of small

00:03:39.450 --> 00:03:45.330
government, libertarian think tanks like it,
which is that they believe minorities are,

00:03:45.330 --> 00:03:53.060
quote, natural Republicans, that if they were
more politically engaged, they would naturally

00:03:53.060 --> 00:03:57.530
go more towards Republicans, partially because
some of them have more religious backgrounds.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:04:03.370
A lot of Catholic Latin American immigrants,
Republicans say, would naturally be Republicans

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.239
if they had a higher voter turnout.

00:04:05.239 --> 00:04:10.210
A lot of socially conservative black Americans
would more naturally go to the Republican

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:17.349
Party if they had higher voter turnout, were
it not for propagandizing and Democrats lying

00:04:17.349 --> 00:04:19.449
to them and belittled all those different
stuff.

00:04:19.449 --> 00:04:21.530
I think the I think it's just a hard no.

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:27.460
I think that, you know, as someone myself
who who is from Argentina and have seen Latin

00:04:27.460 --> 00:04:33.639
American Catholicism firsthand, it is not
the hard right evangelical Christianity that

00:04:33.639 --> 00:04:36.580
leads people to vote for people like Ted Cruz.

00:04:36.580 --> 00:04:41.340
That's not what we're talking about when we
talk about most Latin American Catholics.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:45.819
I don't think there's a natural home in the
Republican Party for that group.

00:04:45.819 --> 00:04:51.380
And I you know, the the idea that black voters
only vote for Democrats because they've been

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:54.719
tricked is also there's absolutely no evidence
behind it whatsoever.

00:04:54.719 --> 00:04:57.449
So I don't buy that hypothesis.

00:04:57.449 --> 00:04:59.520
We will see in 20, 20.

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:03.370
Two and twenty twenty four, what happens with
minority voters?

00:05:03.370 --> 00:05:07.509
My view is we don't have enough information
yet to say whether the gain that Trump saw

00:05:07.509 --> 00:05:13.569
from minority voters in 2020 means anything
long term or if it's a one time change because

00:05:13.569 --> 00:05:16.030
of the particular dynamics of the 2020 election

